    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's expand on the SIR model example posted on 
                Blackboard and discussed in class.  We now have a 
                cure for the Zombies!
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's add the additional assumption that Zombies
                    can be cured and turn back into humans with 
                    parameter <m>c</m>. For now, let us assume this cure
                    happens such that the number of Zombies cured is
                    proportional to the population of Zombies.
                    What are your new differential equations (aka your
                    model)? Hint: add to your schematic!
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Adapt the provided SIR code (or write your own) so 
                    that it solves the new model you have in Part (a).
                    Use the same initial conditions as in the example:
                    <m>{S(0) = 999/1000}</m>, <m>{I(0) = 1/1000}</m>,
                    and <m>{R(0) = 0}</m>.  Use the following parameters 
                    <m>a = 0.003, c = 0.001, d = 0.002,</m> and
                    <m>r = 0.001</m>. Note: these are different 
                    from the model example from class. What happens to 
                    your three populations long term?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What changes in the long term behavior from Part (b)
                    do you see if you change the parameter <m>c</m> to be
                    <m>c = 0.003</m>? Why? That is, what does increasing
                    <m>c</m> do in the context of Zombies?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's go back to the cure and assume that Zombies can only
                    turn back to humans through an encounter between a human
                    and Zombie, where a human administers the cure.  So, instead
                    of the number being proportional to the population of Zombies,
                    the number of Zombies cured is jointly proportional. If we still
                    use the parameter <m>c</m> for this case, what are your new
                    differential equations?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If we use the same parameters as in Part (b), what 
                    happens to the populations in the long term?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What happens if we keep <m>a, d, r</m> the same,
                    but change <m>c</m> to again be <m>c = 0.003</m>.
                    Without graphing the solution, what happens to the
                    susceptible population? How do you know?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>